**Answer: 3**

## Construction (3 points achievable)

Take a triangle with side lengths 3, 5, 7. Since 3 + 5 = 8 > 7, the triangle inequality holds, so three points realizing these side lengths exist in the plane, and every pairwise distance is an odd integer.

## Impossibility of 4 points

Suppose, for contradiction, that four points $P_1, P_2, P_3, P_4$ lie in the plane with every pairwise distance $d_{ij}$ an odd integer. Since the points are coplanar (they lie in $\mathbb{R}^2$), the **Cayley–Menger determinant** must vanish:

$$
\Delta := \det\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\
1 & 0 & d_{12}^2 & d_{13}^2 & d_{14}^2\\
1 & d_{12}^2 & 0 & d_{23}^2 & d_{24}^2\\
1 & d_{13}^2 & d_{23}^2 & 0 & d_{34}^2\\
1 & d_{14}^2 & d_{24}^2 & d_{34}^2 & 0
\end{pmatrix} = 0.
$$

(This determinant equals $288\,V^2$, where $V$ is the volume of the tetrahedron formed by the four points; coplanarity forces $V=0$.)

Now reduce this determinant modulo 8. Every $d_{ij}$ is odd, so $d_{ij}^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$. Substituting, the matrix becomes

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\
1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\
1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\
1 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\
1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0
\end{pmatrix} = J_5 - I_5,
$$

where $J_5$ is the $5\times 5$ all-ones matrix. The eigenvalues of $J_5$ are $5$ (once) and $0$ (four times), so the eigenvalues of $J_5 - I_5$ are $4$ (once) and $-1$ (four times). Hence

$$
\det(J_5 - I_5) = 4\cdot(-1)^4 = 4.
$$

Therefore $\Delta \equiv 4 \pmod{8}$.

But coplanarity requires $\Delta = 0$, and $0 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}$. This contradicts $\Delta \equiv 4 \pmod{8}$.

Hence no four points in the plane can have all pairwise distances odd integers.

## Conclusion

The largest number of points is **3**.

